## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked
Tests nuanced DP on partitioning with range-dependent block costs, requiring problem decomposition, state design, and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and block counts
- Efficient range max computation in transitions
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(nums, m) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, m); t++) {
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i-1; j >= t-1; j--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[j]);
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + curMax * (i - j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct endpoints
  const ends = [];
  let i = n, t = m;
  while (t > 0) {
    ends.push(i);
    i = par[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][m], ends: ends.reverse()};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests weighted interval scheduling with an exact k constraint and reconstruction; requires sorting, precomputing p(i), and a 2D DP with backtracking; also considerations for memory optimization.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling
- DP with an extra dimension for exact k selections
- Precomputing p(i) (latest non-overlapping job)
- Space-time tradeoffs; backtracking reconstruction
- Stability under large n and k; edge-case handling when K > possible non-overlapping sets

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonOverlapping(jobs, K) {
  // jobs: {s, e, r}; sort by end time
  jobs.sort((a,b)=> a.e - b.e);
  const n = jobs.length;
  const end = jobs.map(j=> j.e);
  // p(i): last index < i with end[p] < start[i]
  const p = new Array(n).fill(-1);
  for (let i=0; i<n; i++) {
    let lo=0, hi=i-1, ans=-1;
    while (lo<=hi){
      const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
      if (end[mid] < jobs[i].s){ ans=mid; lo=mid+1; } else hi=mid-1;
    }
    p[i]=ans;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(K+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=1; t<=K; t++){
      const without = dp[i-1][t];
      const withTake = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? dp[p[i-1]+1][t-1] : (t-1===0?0: -Infinity)) + jobs[i-1].r;
      if (withTake > without){ dp[i][t] = withTake; take[i][t] = true; } else { dp[i][t] = without; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  const res = [];
  let i=n, t=K;
  while (i>0 && t>0){
    if (take[i][t]){ res.push(i-1); i = (p[i-1] >= 0 ? p[i-1]+1 : 0); t--; }
    else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return { revenue: dp[n][K], indices: res };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to an "at most K" constraint?
- How can you optimize memory to O(n + K) or O(K) and still reconstruct the solution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a DP with a non-trivial cost function and prefix optimization.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes and partitions
- O(1) substring cost via precomputed diff/pref
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute diff and prefix
let diff = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) diff[i] = s[i-1] !== s[i-2] ? 1:0;
let pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i];

// cost(a,b)
function cost(a,b){ return pref[b] - pref[a]; }

// DP
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = cost(1,i); }

for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
  for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
    for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + cost(p+1,i);
      if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

