## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP over partitions where block value depends on both max and sum, plus reconstruction of segments. Encourages discussion on precomputation of range maxima and efficient transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitioning with fixed number of segments
- Block value using max and sum requires precomputation
- Path reconstruction for exact endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxBlockPartition(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const pref=new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+A[i];
  // precompute max for all subarrays
  const maxSub=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ let m=A[i]; for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ m=Math.max(m,A[j]); maxSub[i][j]=m; } }
  const blockVal=(l,r)=> (r-l+1)*maxSub[l][r] - (pref[r+1]-pref[l]);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par=Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t]=[i-1,t];
      for(let s=1;s<=i;s++){
        const val=dp[s-1][t-1] + blockVal(s-1,i-1);
        if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=[s-1,t-1]; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack from (n,k) using par
  return {best: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to avoid O(n^2 k) in practice?  
- How does reconstruction behave with negative block values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over multiple segments with a budget and in/out state tracking to allow adjacency.

## Key Concepts
- DP with segment count and in/out state
- Transitions for starting, extending, and ending a segment
- Handling adjacency and at-most-k constraint

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(a, k){
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  // dp[i][t][s] -> max sum using first i elements
  let dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array.from({length: k+1}, ()=> [NEG, NEG]));
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      for(let s=0;s<2;s++){
        const cur = dp[i][t][s];
        if(cur===NEG) continue;
        if(s===0){
          // skip a[i]
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
          // start new
          if(t<k) dp[i+1][t+1][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t+1][1], cur + a[i]);
        } else {
          // extend
          dp[i+1][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][1], cur + a[i]);
          // end
          dp[i+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i+1][t][0], cur);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[n][t][0], dp[n][t][1]);
  return ans;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests building a DP over subsequences with constraints, requiring a clear state definition and transition over previous indices, plus reconstruction capabilities.

## Key Concepts
- Subsequence DP with constraints
- State dp[i] and parent tracking
- Time/space analysis and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSubseqWithDiff(a, d) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(0);
  const par = Array(n).fill(-1);
  let best = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    dp[i] = a[i];
    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
      if (Math.abs(a[i] - a[j]) <= d && dp[j] + a[i] > dp[i]) {
        dp[i] = dp[j] + a[i];
        par[i] = j;
      }
    }
    if (dp[i] > best) {
      best = dp[i];
      bestIdx = i;
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct subsequence
  const seq = [];
  let curr = bestIdx;
  while (curr !== -1) {
    seq.push(curr);
    curr = par[curr];
  }
  return { maxSum: best, subsequence: seq.reverse() };
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity
Time: O(n²) due to nested loops checking all previous indices
Space: O(n) for dp and parent arrays

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests the ability to formulate a DP solution over prefixes with a partition constraint, combining range-based computations (maximum values) with block length considerations. It requires clear state definition, proper transition logic, and a strategy for reconstructing the solution from computed results.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over array prefixes and block count parameters
- State transitions focusing on the last block contribution
- Efficient handling of range maximum queries during transitions
- Solution reconstruction using parent pointers or backtracking
- Time-space trade-offs and potential RMQ-based optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// DP framework structure
function partitionArray(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array(n+1).fill().map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  // Base cases: single block
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    const maxVal = Math.max(...A.slice(0, i));
    dp[i][1] = maxVal * i;
  }

  // Fill DP table
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t-1; j < i; j++) {
        const maxLast = Math.max(...A.slice(j, i));
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + maxLast * (i-j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n, blocksLeft = k;
  while (blocksLeft > 0) {
    const start = par[curr][blocksLeft];
    blocks.push([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
    blocksLeft--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks: blocks.reverse() };
}
```

